Charter, Art. 1 — The right to self-determination

do not have unlimited “substitute” decision-making power: the older adult, when capable, remains the primary recipient of information, while the family member plays a role of mediation, clarification, support, and, if necessary, formal representation.

“Free, full, and informed self-determination”

The stated goal is for the elderly person to be able to self-determine, that is, to participate in a substantially autonomous manner in the choices that concern them, not merely to “sign off” on what others have decided. For this self-determination to be genuine, the information must be:

complete with regard to alternatives and foreseeable consequences;

understandable (appropriate to the cultural level, non-technical language, possible use of written or visual aids);

dialogic, that is, the result of a process of questions and answers, not one-way communication.

Older adults have the right to seek support and assistance from people they trust and choose when making financial decisions.

Especially when faced with cognitive challenges, older adults need support

to improve their levels of “financial literacy,” so they can understand the legal and financial implications and make informed decisions regarding health issues, the death of a relative, or moving into a care facility. This is particularly important because it allows older adults to retain control over their finances and remain as independent as possible in their daily lives.

There are some real obstacles to this right:

Global digital divide: Across all continents, low digital literacy remains one of the main obstacles. Many older adults lack access to devices, the internet, or digital assistance, or face difficulties learning and adapting to new technologies such as telehealth, health apps, information portals, and online services;

Sensory and cognitive barriers: visual, hearing, motor, and cognitive impairments (memory decline, dementia, low levels of functional literacy) limit the ability to understand information, read medical documents, follow procedures, and use digital tools, everywhere in the world;

Organizational and continuity issues: Globally, the healthcare system is often fragmented, with a lack of coordination between primary care physicians, hospitals, and social and community services. In wealthy countries, the problem stems from bureaucracy; in

poorer countries, it involves a shortage of staff and structured services;

Misinformation, stigma, and social isolation: the lack of clear information, widespread stigma toward old age, and loneliness limit older adults’ ability to take initiative. In some regions, language and cultural barriers also emerge, further complicating access to information services.

Charter, Art. 7 — The right to a safe living space

In the United States, approximately 27% of people aged 60 and older live alone—one of the highest rates in the world—and in several European countries, the figures are even higher; where households are smaller, the likelihood that older adults will grow old in domestic solitude is greater.

In Italy, there are approximately 9. million single-person households (2021), and 42.6% of these consist of people aged 65 or older. This means that there are around 4 million older adults living alone; ISTAT projections indicate that the number of older adults living alone will grow to approximately 6. million by 2050.

In Japan in 2020, there were approximately 6. million single-person households of older adults (65+). According to official projections, households consisting of a single

elderly person will reach approximately 10. million by 2050, accounting for over 20% of all Japanese households.

This combination of single-person households and loneliness is associated with a higher risk of depression, functional decline, increased demand for residential care, and mortality; loneliness among those over 55 is now also recognized as an economic problem, with healthcare costs estimated in the billions each year.

Why supportive cohousing is a cross-cutting solution

Research on cohousing for the elderly and on shared living models shows that these solutions:189

reduce loneliness and isolation compared to living alone;

improve quality of life, sense of security, and mutual support;

facilitate aging in place, that is, the ability to remain independent for longer. For older adults living alone in developed countries, supportive cohousing offers:190 – a daily environment of stable relationships, shared meals

, and mutual assistance;

economies of scale on rent, utilities, and small care services;

the possibility of avoiding, or at least postponing, the need for residential care facilities.

For older adults in resource-limited countries, adapting the concept of cohousing means:191

creating micro-communities where housing and service costs are shared and more sustainable;

organizing mutual aid and intergenerational support networks in contexts where public services are scarce;

offering spaces that are safer and more livable than many informal dwellings, without severing ties with the local community.

In both cases, cohousing does not replace the family, but rather reconstructs a family-like environment where the biological family is absent, reduced, or in difficulty, and allows the older person, if they so desire, to experience daily relationships based not only on care but also on reciprocity.

